  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "In a red-blue tree, each vertex is either red or blue and adjacent vertices always have different colours.\n\nYou are given a tree with NNN vertices (numbered 111 through NNN). It is not necessarily a red-blue tree, but its vertices are still coloured red and blue. You may perform the following operation any number of times (including zero): choose two adjacent vertices and swap their colours.\n\nFind the smallest number of operations required to transform the tree into a red-blue tree or determine that it is impossible.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1001 \\le T \\le 1001≤T≤100",
      "1≤N≤100,0001 \\le N \\le 100,0001≤N≤100,000",
      "1≤u,v≤N1 \\le u, v \\le N1≤u,v≤N",
      "SSS contains only characters '0' and '1'",
      "the sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 10610^6106"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "The first line of the input contains a single integer TTT denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follows.\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN.\nN−1N-1N−1 lines follow. Each of these lines contains two space-separated integers uuu and vvv denoting that vertices uuu and vvv are connected by an edge.\nThe last line contains a string SSS with length NNN. For each valid iii, the iii-th character of this string is either '0' if the iii-th vertex is initially red or '1' if it is initially blue.",
    "outputFormat": "Print a single line containing one integer ― the smallest number of operations or −1-1−1 if it is impossible to transform the tree into a red-blue tree.",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "1  \n7  \n1 2  \n1 3  \n2 4  \n2 5  \n3 6  \n3 7  \n0010010",
        "output": "3",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nExample case 1: We can perform the following operations:\n\nSwap the colours of vertices 111 and 333; the string of colours becomes \"1000010\".\nSwap the colours of vertices 111 and 222; the string of colours becomes \"0100010\".\nSwap the colours of vertices 666 and 333; the string of colours becomes \"0110000\", so the tree is a red-blue tree."
      }
    ]
  },
